 ## Context
 
-Technicians enter an equipment serial number and need its calibration date, due date, and equipment description. A signed snapshot contains newline-delimited JSON records, a schema version, a snapshot date, and a signature manifest.
+Technicians enter an equipment serial number and need its calibration date, due date, and equipment description. A signed snapshot contains newline-delimited JSON records, a schema version, a snapshot date, and a signature manifest. Snapshots arrive on approved USB media; during normal operation a kiosk has no network route.
 
 ## Architecture
 
 Expose `findBySerial(serial)` returning either `Found(record, snapshotDate)` or `NotFound(snapshotDate)`. Record fields and result types are fixed regardless of the lookup backend. The certificates node uses only this interface.
 
-Snapshot validation, backend preparation, and lookup all belong to this node. The lookup backend is not selected yet. Candidates are a local embedded SQLite index, an on-site HTTPS directory, and a managed search service. No certificate behavior or interface change is part of that selection.
+Lookup is served by a local embedded SQLite index built from the active snapshot at import time. Snapshot validation, index build, activation, and lookup all belong to this node. The index is derived data: the validated snapshot is the source of truth and the index can be rebuilt from it. No service and no network access are involved in lookup, so the kiosk can resolve serial numbers while isolated.
 
 ## Snapshot Import
 
-Verify the signature using a bundled public key, reject unsupported schema versions, and validate record serial numbers for uniqueness. Prepare the new snapshot without exposing partially imported records. Activate it only after all validation succeeds. An import failure leaves the previous snapshot active and reports a useful error.
+Verify the signature using a bundled public key, reject unsupported schema versions, and validate record serial numbers for uniqueness. Build a new SQLite index in a staging location from the validated records, then activate it only once the whole index is built. Activation makes the new index and its snapshot date visible to lookups as a single switch; the previous index remains served until that switch completes. An import failure leaves the previous snapshot active, discards the staged index, and reports a useful error.
+
+## Index Build and Activation
+
+The index is a SQLite database in the kiosk's registry data directory. It holds one row per validated record keyed by the normalized serial number (see Lookup Behavior), carrying the record's display serial number, equipment description, calibration date, and due date, plus a metadata row holding the snapshot date. Build runs inside a single transaction so an interrupted build never yields a usable-looking index.
+
+Activation replaces the active index with the staged build, so lookups observe either the old index and date or the new index and date, never a mixture and never a partial index. The staged build and the active index live on the same filesystem so the replacement can be atomic.
 
 ## Lookup Behavior
 
-Normalize surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters before lookup. Serial-number punctuation remains significant. Return the snapshot date even when no matching record exists. The concrete index and query path remain open pending the user's choice.
+Normalize surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters before lookup; serial-number punctuation remains significant. The index is keyed by the normalized serial number and the query is normalized the same way, so a serial matches regardless of the casing or surrounding whitespace in the query or in the stored record. Return the snapshot date from the active index's metadata even when no matching record exists.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Lookup backend | Local embedded SQLite index | On-site HTTPS directory; managed search service | Both alternatives require runtime network access, which the kiosk does not have during normal operation. SQLite keeps lookups predictable and self-contained, at the cost of each kiosk rebuilding its own index when a snapshot arrives. |
+| Index role | Derived data rebuilt from the validated snapshot | Index as a second source of truth | Keeping the validated snapshot authoritative lets a corrupt or lost index be rebuilt without re-importing from USB, and confines index format changes to a rebuild. |
+| Index key | Normalized serial number | Raw serial number | Keying on the normalized form makes lookups insensitive to case and surrounding whitespace consistently on both the build and query sides. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Deferred
+
+1. Whether uniqueness validation operates on the raw or the normalized serial number (two raw serials that normalize to the same key are currently undecided).
+2. Whether an unreadable or corrupt active index surfaces a distinct lookup error rather than `NotFound`.
+3. Recovery and cleanup of an orphaned staged index left by a crash mid-build.
